          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Nikki\",\"addressee\":\"Keith\",\"anchor_quote\":\"I love you\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Nikki directly avows love as her present emotional state. The narration also characterizes the utterance as an honest admission, making truth-presenting avowal its dominant force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty that left them both vulnerable but deeply connected.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal words contain no promise, proposal, obligation, or specified commitment by Nikki. Their intimate setting and Keith's reciprocal avowal do not convert Nikki's declaration into an undertaking about their future.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Keith stilled, then smiled. “I love you too,” he said, his voice steady and sincere.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Alongside being an avowal, the expression is represented as a spontaneous, difficult-to-control outpouring of affect: it emerges suddenly and spills out before Nikki can restrain it. That gives it substantial expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"she said suddenly, the words spilling out before she could stop them.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"The admission felt like a release\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"No core function beyond the loving-state avowal and its spontaneous expressive discharge is supported. In particular, the expression is not itself a request, command, question, or distinct social act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Nikki says the words suddenly to Keith while they are lying together in bed after an intimate encounter. Keith pauses, smiles, and reciprocates the avowal.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Nikki undertakes no explicit obligation in the focal expression. Keith responds with a reciprocal declaration—“I love you too”—which acknowledges and returns the avowal, but no concrete relational terms or future commitment are negotiated here.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize the supplied text as a specific known work.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports Nikki's contemporaneous affection and the spontaneous character of this utterance, but it does not establish precise relationship terms, durability, or any commitment implicit beyond the declaration itself. The supplied later material is discontinuous and cannot fill those gaps.\"}"
